node. To avoid scanning every time, we can maintain counts of nodes per symbol that are still retained. Initially count(symbol) = number of nodes with that symbol. When we remove a node, we decrement count for that node's symbol. So we can check that for each required symbol, count[symbol] - (if node.symbol == symbol then 1 else 0) > 0? Actually careful: removal of a node removes its entire subtree, not just the node. So we need to account for all symbols in the subtree. So we cannot just use a simple decrement of one node. Better to compute subtree symbol counts for each node. We can precompute for each node: a dict mapping symbol to count in its subtree. Then for removal candidate, we check for each required symbol: total_count[symbol] - subtree_count[symbol] >= 1? Actually we need at least one retained node after removal. The retained nodes are all nodes except those in the removed subtree. So we can check for each required symbol s: (total_initial_count[s] - subtree_count_of_node[s]) >= 1. We can precompute subtree counts using DFS.\n\n   - Also note: The root node is not removable, but its symbol is likely \"root\". required_symbols may not include \"root\"; but root is retained always.\n\n   - Also, if removal condition holds, we add node id to removed_ids (in order of processing), and update retained set accordingly. But note: we are processing nodes in order; once we decide to remove a node, we should also mark its entire subtree as removed. However, if we process deeper nodes first, we will have already processed all descendants of this node. For those descendants, if we already decided to remove them (if they were removable), they are already in removed_ids. But if they were not removable, then we cannot remove this node because children not removed. So we only remove a node when all children are already removed. So when we remove the node, we add it to removed_ids, and the subtree is effectively removed because children are already removed. So we don't need to recursively mark.\n\n   - For checking \"retained children\": we need to check that all children are in removed_ids. For nodes that have children that are not removable and thus never get removed, the parent cannot be removed. So condition: for each child_id in children[node.id], child_id must be in removed_ids. This ensures no retained children.\n\n   - Also need to ensure that node itself is not a required symbol node. So if node.symbol in required_symbols, skip.\n\n7. After processing all nodes, removed_node_ids list is in the order of removal (which is the order we iterated and decided to remove). retained_node_ids = all node ids not in removed_ids, sorted lexicographically (since IDs are strings, default sort works).\n\n8. Return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_node_ids, \"retained_node_ids\": sorted_retained}.\n\nWe must implement as pure Python, no imports beyond stdlib. Use only case fields.\n\nPotential edge cases: nodes with parent null but multiple roots? In data, only one root with parent null. If multiple, we need to handle? Probably not.\n\nAlso required_symbols may contain symbols that appear multiple times. Ensure after removal, at least one copy remains.\n\nWe'll need to compute subtree symbol counts. We can build tree adjacency, then do DFS from root to compute subtree_counts dict: for each node, key=id, value=dict{symbol: count in subtree}. But careful: nodes have depth field; we can use that for ordering. Or we can just sort nodes by depth descending and id ascending.\n\nImplementation plan:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])  # set for fast check\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\n    children = {}\n    for n in nodes:\n        pid = n['parent']\n        if pid is not None:\n            children.setdefault(pid, []).append(n['id'])\n    # compute subtree symbol counts via postorder DFS\n    # We'll compute for all nodes, using recursion or stack. Use recursion? Depth moderate.\n    # We'll compute subtree_counts = {}\n    # Do DFS from root. Root id? Find node with parent None.\n    root = None\n    for n in nodes:\n        if n['parent'] is None:\n            root = n['id']\n            break\n    assert root is not None\n    subtree_counts = {}\n    def dfs(node_id):\n        counts = {}\n        # add own symbol\n        sym = node_map[node_id]['symbol']\n        counts[sym] = counts.get(sym, 0) + 1\n        for child_id in children.get(node_id, []):\n            child_counts = dfs(child_id)\n            for sym2, c in child_counts.items():\n                counts[sym2] = counts.get(sym2, 0) + c\n        subtree_counts[node_id] = counts\n        return counts\n    dfs(root)\n    \n    # initial total counts (from root subtree)\n    total_counts = subtree_counts[root]\n    \n    removed_ids = set()\n    removed_order = []\n    \n    # sort nodes: depth descending, then id ascending\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\n    \n    for n in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = n['id']\n        if not n['removable']:\n            continue\n        if nid == root:\n            continue\n        if n['symbol'] in required_symbols:\n            continue\n        # check all children are already removed\n        child_ids = children.get(nid, [])\n        if any(cid not in removed_ids for cid in child_ids):\n            continue\n        # check that removing this node (and its subtree) leaves all required symbols\n        # subtree symbols count for this node\n        sub_counts = subtree_counts[nid]\n        feasible = True\n        for req_sym in required_symbols:\n            remaining = total_counts.get(req_sym, 0) - sub_counts.get(req_sym, 0)\n            if remaining < 1:\n                feasible = False\n                break\n        if not feasible:\n            continue\n        # perform removal\n        removed_ids.add(nid)\n        removed_order.append(nid)\n    \n    retained_ids = [n['id'] for n in nodes if n['id'] not in removed_ids]\n    retained_ids.sort()\n    \n    return {\n        'removed_node_ids': removed_order,\n        'retained_node_ids': retained_ids\n    }\n```\n\nCheck invariants: We used total_counts from root which includes all nodes. When we remove a node, we do not update total_counts; we only use subtraction check. That's fine because we only ever check for removal once per node, and we don't remove nodes that have non-removed children, so we are essentially removing subtrees from the leaves up. Since we don't remove nodes that would invalidate required symbols, we never remove a required symbol's only occurrence. The order of removal is deterministic: depth desc, id asc. That matches \"deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes\" and \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth\". Also \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" We provide removed_order as list of ids in removal order.\n\nEdge: required_symbols may include symbol that appears only in root. Root is not removable, so fine. Also if required_symbols includes a symbol that only appears in a node that is removable and we might remove it, but our check prevents that.\n\nAlso note: The description says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" We implement required symbol check as node's symbol in required_symbols. But what if a node's symbol is not a required symbol, but its descendant has a required symbol? That's handled by the subtree check.\n\nPotential issue: If a node has required symbol in its subtree but also elsewhere, removal allowed if other representation remains. That's fine.\n\nAlso we must validate input: raise ValueError if something is structurally invalid? But we can assume valid per fixture. However, we could add a check: if root node missing or required_symbol empty? But not needed.\n\nOne more nuance: The description says \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" That means after removal, the remaining tree must have all required symbols. Our check does that.\n\nAlso \"Do not remove ... a node with retained children.\" We check that all children are in removed_ids. But if a node has no children, it's fine.\n\nMake sure to handle case where a node has children that are not removable and thus never removed. Then parent cannot be removed because child not removed. That's correct.\n\nTesting with the given fixtures: We can simulate mentally.\n\nFor case 0:\nnodes: root (depth0, removable false), n1 (depth1, removable false, symbol s1), n2 (depth1, removable true, symbol s2), n3 (depth1, removable false, symbol s0), n4 (depth2, removable true, symbol s1), n5 (depth2, removable false, symbol s2), n6 (depth2, removable true, symbol s0). required_symbols: root, s1.\n- Sort depth desc: n4, n5, n6 (depth2) then n1,n2,n3 (depth1) then root (depth0). For depth2 tie: id order: 'n4','n5','n6'.\nProcess n4: removable true, symbol s1 which is required -> skip. n5: removable false -> skip. n6: removable true, symbol s0 not required, children? none. Check subtree counts: n6 subtree has s0 only. total_counts: root s0 from n3 and n6? Actually root has? root symbol root; n1 s1; n2 s2; n3 s0; n4 s1; n5 s2; n6 s0. So total s0 count = 2 (n3 and n6), s1 count=2 (n1,n4), s2=2 (n2,n5), root=1. For n6 removal, sub_counts={s0:1}. For required s1: remaining s1 count =2-0=2 >=1 ok; for root: remaining root=1-0=1 ok. So remove n6. removed_ids={n6}, removed_order=[n6].\nNext depth1: nodes n1, n2, n3 sorted by id: n1, n2, n3.\nn1: removable false, skip.\nn2: removable true, symbol s2 not required. Children: n4. Is n4 in removed_ids? n4 not removed yet. So has retained child (n4) -> skip.\nn3: removable false, skip.\nNow depth0: root: not removable, skip.\nSecond iteration? No, all processed. So only n6 removed. retained: all others sorted: ['n1','n2','n3','n4','n5','root']? Actually root id is 'root' and nodes sorted: 'n1','n2','n3